The Python literal "'\";\\--" is the 6-character string: ' " ; \ - - i.e. the distinct character set {', ", ;, \, -}. `ch in term` iterates that set character-by-character, so any occurrence of a single quote, double quote, semicolon, backslash or hyphen raises.

The payload q = "' OR 1=1 -- " begins with a single quote (and also contains two hyphens), so the very first iteration ch=="'" matches and ValueError is raised. run_query() at line 34 is never reached.

Independently of the payload, this layer removes the only character that can terminate the single-quoted LIKE literal at line 34 ("...LIKE '%" + term + "%'"): the single quote, and also the backslash. What survives is a strict subset of characters that cannot break out of the literal (%, _ wildcards at most, which alter matching but not SQL structure).

Layer 1 (ALLOWED_TERM = \A[A-Za-z0-9 _-]{1,64}\Z, line 20) also rejects this payload — the quote and '=' are outside the class — so the payload fails both layers; but the verdict for the layer under test stands on its own.
